\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The open Clifford quotient and locally constant spinor norms}

\p{Let #{K}, #{V}, #{Q} and their topologies be as in \ref{fcap-0045}.
The continuous surjection
#{\tau:\Gamma(Q)\to O(Q)} is open and is a quotient map. Give
#{\mathcal S_K=K^{\times}/(K^{\times})^2} its quotient topology. It is
discrete, and the two spinor norms of \ref{fcap-002D},

##{\begin{aligned}
\nu_Q^O &: O(Q)\longrightarrow\mathcal S_K,\\
\nu_Q &: SO(Q)\longrightarrow\mathcal S_K,
\end{aligned}}

are continuous and locally constant. In particular,
#{\rho(\Spin(Q))=\ker\nu_Q} is an open subgroup of #{SO(Q)}. This says
that sufficiently small changes of an isometry preserve its square-class
obstruction to a Spin lift.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{First suppose #{\dim_K V>0}. Local fields are locally compact and
#{\sigma}-compact, meaning a countable union of compact subsets. For a
nonarchimedean local field, its valuation ring is compact: completeness
and the finite residue quotients make it a complete totally bounded
metric space. Its scalar dilates exhaust #{K}; ordinary closed balls
give the analogous exhaustion for #{\mathbb R} and #{\mathbb C}.
Finite products give compact exhaustions of #{A=\Cl(Q)} and #{A^2}.}

\p{By \ref{fcap-0044}, the units identify homeomorphically with the
closed inverse graph #{\{(x,y):xy=yx=1\}\subseteq A^2}. Hence
#{A^\times} is locally compact and #{\sigma}-compact. The closedness
of #{\Gamma(Q)} in \ref{fcap-0045} gives the same properties to
#{\Gamma(Q)}. Its vector action is continuous there and onto by
\ref{fcap-002D}; the target #{O(Q)} is locally compact Hausdorff.}

\p{Here is the open-map argument. Given an open identity neighborhood
#{U\subseteq\Gamma(Q)}, choose an open identity neighborhood #{W}
with compact closure and
#{\overline W\,\overline W^{-1}\subseteq U}. Such #{W} exists by
continuity of multiplication and inversion and local compactness.
A compact exhaustion and finite subcovers of its terms give a countable
cover #{\Gamma(Q)=\bigcup_j g_jW}. Thus

##{O(Q)=\bigcup_j\tau(g_j)\tau(\overline W).}

Each set in this union is closed, because #{\tau(\overline W)} is
compact and the target is Hausdorff. The Baire theorem for locally
compact Hausdorff spaces implies that #{\tau(\overline W)} has nonempty
interior. If #{D} is a nonempty open subset of this image and #{d\in D},
then #{Dd^{-1}} is an open identity neighborhood contained in

##{\tau(\overline W)\tau(\overline W)^{-1}\subseteq\tau(U).}

Translating this conclusion around every point of any open subset of
#{\Gamma(Q)} proves that #{\tau} is open. A continuous open surjection
is a quotient map: a subset of the target whose inverse image is open
is the open image of that inverse image. This is the argument underlying
Mathlib's \leanref{MonoidHom.isOpenMap_of_sigmaCompact}.}

\p{The reverse norm #{N_{\mathrm{rev}}:\Gamma(Q)\to K^\times}
is continuous. Indeed, \ref{fcap-002D} identifies
#{\operatorname{rev}(x)x} with the scalar #{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(x)};
extracting its scalar coefficient is a linear map on #{A}. Its values
are nonzero, and #{K^\times} has the subspace topology from #{K} by
\ref{fcap-0044}.}

\p{The square subgroup is open in #{K^\times}. Over #{\mathbb R} it is
the positive half-line, and over #{\mathbb C} it is all of #{K^\times}.
For a complete discretely valued field of characteristic different from
two, let #{\mathcal O_K} be its valuation ring and #{\mathfrak m_K}
its maximal ideal. The local square theorem gives

##{1+4\mathfrak m_K\subseteq(K^\times)^2.}

This is \citet{VI.2, Theorem 2.19 and Corollary 2.20, p. 161}{lam2005quadratic};
its complete discrete valuation hypotheses apply also in residue
characteristic two. Since #{4\ne0}, multiplication by #{4} is a
homeomorphism, so the left side is an open identity neighborhood.
Translating it by squares proves that the subgroup is open. Every square
coset is therefore open, which makes its quotient #{\mathcal S_K}
discrete.}

\p{Let #{q:K^\times\to\mathcal S_K} be the quotient map. The norm
identity in \ref{fcap-002D} is

##{\nu_Q^O\circ\tau=q\circ N_{\mathrm{rev}}.}

The right side is continuous. The established quotient property of
#{\tau} therefore proves continuity of #{\nu_Q^O}; restricting to the
subgroup #{SO(Q)} proves continuity of #{\nu_Q}. A continuous map to a
discrete space is locally constant. More explicitly,

##{\ker\nu_Q^O=\tau\bigl(N_{\mathrm{rev}}^{-1}((K^\times)^2)\bigr)}

is open, since both the preimage and #{\tau} are open. Its intersection
with #{SO(Q)} is #{\ker\nu_Q}, identified with the Spin image by
\ref{fcap-0023}. In dimension zero all domain groups are trivial by
\ref{fcap-0045}; the same square-class topology and conclusions apply.}
}

\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isOpenMap_lipschitzToOrthogonal}
uses a Hausdorff, locally compact, #{\sigma}-compact topological field.
Its \leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.continuous_cliffordNorm} uses a
complete nontrivially normed field. Finally,
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.continuous_orthogonalSpinorNorm},
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.continuous_spinorNorm} and
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.isOpen_range_spinToSpecialOrthogonal}
assume a locally compact nontrivially normed field and explicitly require
open squares. Each declaration assumes finite dimension and invertible
two, with nondegeneracy for the action and spinor-norm conclusions.
The local fields here satisfy those hypotheses, and the cited local
square theorem discharges the extra open-squares condition.}
